### What is the Subset Sum Problem?

Given a set of positive numbers and a target S, determine whether any subset of the numbers sums to exactly S. This is a decision problem: the answer is true or false.

At National Bank of India, the fraud team has a list of individual charges on a customer's account and a disputed total. They need to know if some combination of the charges exactly matches the disputed amount.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class SubsetSum {
    public static boolean canReachTarget(int[] nums, int target) {
        boolean[] dp = new boolean[target + 1];
        dp[0] = true;  // empty subset sums to zero

        for (int num : nums) {
            // Sweep from high to low to ensure each number used at most once
            for (int t = target; t >= num; t--) {
                if (dp[t - num]) {
                    dp[t] = true;
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[target];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] charges = {3000, 34000, 4000, 12000, 5000, 2000};
        int disputed = 9000;
        System.out.println("Can reach " + disputed + ": " + canReachTarget(charges, disputed));
        // 4000 + 5000 = 9000 -> true
    }
}
```

```text
Can reach 9000: true
```

### Why Does This Problem Matter?

Subset Sum is the pure feasibility variant of the 0/1 Knapsack. Understanding it unlocks a family of related problems: equal partition, minimum subset sum difference, target sum with plus and minus, and count of subsets with a given sum. It is also a classic NP-complete problem, which means no polynomial-time algorithm is known for arbitrary inputs.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class SubsetSumFamily {
    // Subset Sum: can we hit target S? (this problem)
    // Equal Partition: can we split into two equal halves? -> Subset Sum with S = total/2
    // Min Subset Sum Diff: minimize |sum1 - sum2| -> find largest reachable sum <= total/2
    // Count Subsets: how many subsets sum to S? -> change boolean to int count
}
```

### How Does the Boolean DP Work?

We maintain a boolean array `dp` of size S+1, where `dp[t]` is true when some subset of the numbers processed so far sums to exactly t. We seed `dp[0] = true` because the empty subset sums to zero. For each number, we check whether adding it to an already-reachable sum creates a new reachable sum.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SubsetSumTrace {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums = {3, 4, 5};
        int target = 9;
        boolean[] dp = new boolean[target + 1];
        dp[0] = true;

        for (int num : nums) {
            System.out.println("Processing number: " + num);
            for (int t = target; t >= num; t--) {
                if (dp[t - num] && !dp[t]) {
                    dp[t] = true;
                    System.out.println("  Marked dp[" + t + "] = true (from dp[" + (t - num) + "])");
                }
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Can reach " + target + ": " + dp[target]);
    }
}
```

```text
Processing number: 3
  Marked dp[3] = true (from dp[0])
Processing number: 4
  Marked dp[7] = true (from dp[3])
  Marked dp[4] = true (from dp[0])
Processing number: 5
  Marked dp[9] = true (from dp[4])
  Marked dp[8] = true (from dp[3])
  Marked dp[5] = true (from dp[0])
Can reach 9: true
```

### Why Must the Sweep Go from High to Low?

The high-to-low sweep is the correctness guard that makes this 0/1 rather than unbounded. If we swept from low to high, a single number could be added to a sum it just created in the same pass, effectively using it multiple times.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SweepDirection {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums = {3};
        int target = 9;

        // WRONG: low to high allows reuse
        boolean[] dpWrong = new boolean[target + 1];
        dpWrong[0] = true;
        for (int t = 3; t <= target; t++) {
            if (dpWrong[t - 3]) dpWrong[t] = true;
        }
        System.out.println("Low-to-high dp[9] = " + dpWrong[9]);  // true (WRONG: 3 used 3 times)

        // CORRECT: high to low prevents reuse
        boolean[] dpRight = new boolean[target + 1];
        dpRight[0] = true;
        for (int t = target; t >= 3; t--) {
            if (dpRight[t - 3]) dpRight[t] = true;
        }
        System.out.println("High-to-low dp[9] = " + dpRight[9]);  // false (CORRECT: only one 3)
    }
}
```

```text
Low-to-high dp[9] = true
High-to-low dp[9] = false
```

### How Is This Different from the 2D Approach?

The full 2D approach uses a table `dp[i][t]` where i is the number of items considered and t is the target sum. The 1D single-row approach optimizes space by observing that each row only depends on the previous row, so we can overwrite in place with the high-to-low sweep.

| Aspect | 2D Table | 1D Array |
|---|---|---|
| Space | O(N * S) | O(S) |
| Time | O(N * S) | O(N * S) |
| Reconstruction | Can trace back which items were chosen | Cannot trace without extra storage |
| Implementation | Two nested loops, clear dependency | Single array, must sweep high-to-low |

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SubsetSum2D {
    public static boolean canReach2D(int[] nums, int target) {
        int n = nums.length;
        boolean[][] dp = new boolean[n + 1][target + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = true;

        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            for (int t = 1; t <= target; t++) {
                dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];  // skip current number
                if (t >= nums[i - 1]) {
                    dp[i][t] = dp[i][t] || dp[i - 1][t - nums[i - 1]];  // take it
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[n][target];
    }
}
```

### What Are the Edge Cases?

The edge cases include: target is zero (always true via empty subset), target exceeds the total of all numbers (immediately false), all numbers are larger than the target (only dp[0] is true), and a single number equal to the target.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SubsetSumEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Target 0: always true
        System.out.println(SubsetSum.canReachTarget(new int[]{5, 10}, 0));  // true

        // Target > total: false
        System.out.println(SubsetSum.canReachTarget(new int[]{1, 2, 3}, 100));  // false

        // Single number equals target
        System.out.println(SubsetSum.canReachTarget(new int[]{7}, 7));  // true

        // All numbers larger than target
        System.out.println(SubsetSum.canReachTarget(new int[]{10, 20}, 5));  // false
    }
}
```

```text
true
false
true
false
```

### What About Early Termination?

We can exit early as soon as `dp[target]` becomes true, since we only need to know if the target is reachable. We can also check at the start whether the target exceeds the total sum of all numbers, returning false immediately.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SubsetSumEarlyExit {
    public static boolean canReachFast(int[] nums, int target) {
        int total = 0;
        for (int n : nums) total += n;
        if (target > total) return false;  // impossible

        boolean[] dp = new boolean[target + 1];
        dp[0] = true;

        for (int num : nums) {
            for (int t = target; t >= num; t--) {
                if (dp[t - num]) dp[t] = true;
            }
            if (dp[target]) return true;  // early exit
        }
        return dp[target];
    }
}
```

### What Are Common Interview Follow-Up Questions?

Interviewers commonly ask:

- Equal Partition: can the array be split into two subsets with equal sum? This is Subset Sum with target = total / 2.
- Count the number of subsets that sum to S: change the boolean array to an integer count array.
- Reconstruct which numbers form the subset: use the 2D table and trace back.
- What if numbers can be negative? The standard approach assumes positive numbers; negatives require shifting the index range.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class EqualPartition {
    public static boolean canPartition(int[] nums) {
        int total = 0;
        for (int n : nums) total += n;
        if (total % 2 != 0) return false;  // odd total cannot split evenly
        return SubsetSum.canReachTarget(nums, total / 2);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] amounts = {1000, 5000, 11000, 5000};
        System.out.println("Can partition: " + canPartition(amounts));
        // 1000 + 5000 + 5000 = 11000 = other half -> true
    }
}
```

```text
Can partition: true
```
